1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,744
Last in the voice module: putting an actual character on screen instead of the default orb.

2
00:00:02,744 --> 00:00:06,000
We'll load one — and cover the licensing question honestly, because it matters if you're recording publicly.

3
00:00:06,000 --> 00:00:08,510
In Settings, Voice, there's an On-screen character option.

4
00:00:08,510 --> 00:00:13,356
Switch it from the orb to a VRM model and you've got a proper 3D avatar that moves and lip-syncs while it talks.

5
00:00:13,356 --> 00:00:15,000
It's the same call — just with a face.

6
00:00:15,000 --> 00:00:16,355
Where do models come from?

7
00:00:16,355 --> 00:00:21,829
Two easy sources: VRoid Hub, and the app's built-in Open Source Avatars list, which is browsable and CC0.

8
00:00:21,829 --> 00:00:26,000
Pick one and hit Import — it's copied into your avatars folder and ready to use.

9
00:00:26,000 --> 00:00:30,810
If you want an MMD model instead — the pmx format — there's one wrinkle: it needs its texture folder alongside it to render.

10
00:00:30,810 --> 00:00:35,000
So you import MMD models as a zip that contains the model and its textures together, and the app unpacks it.

11
00:00:35,000 --> 00:00:36,653
Now the part people skip and regret: licensing.

12
00:00:36,653 --> 00:00:41,156
VRoid Hub models carry per-creator conditions — some forbid commercial use, some forbid modification, some forbid corporate use.

13
00:00:41,156 --> 00:00:43,794
If you're putting a video on YouTube, that's a use you must be cleared for.

14
00:00:43,794 --> 00:00:46,749
The safe defaults are CC0 sources, or a character you make yourself in VRoid Studio.

15
00:00:46,749 --> 00:00:49,000
When in doubt, don't put someone else's model in a public video.

16
00:00:49,000 --> 00:00:51,414
Two things to set expectations, so you don't troubleshoot non-problems.

17
00:00:51,414 --> 00:00:56,853
Mouth movement follows the volume of the speech, not the actual phonemes — so it's an approximation, not perfect lip-sync; that's a known limitation, not a bug.

18
00:00:56,853 --> 00:01:01,000
And on a laptop, a 3D avatar is real work for the graphics chip: switch back to the orb on battery or integrated graphics.

19
00:01:01,000 --> 00:01:02,178
That completes the voice module.

20
00:01:02,178 --> 00:01:06,485
Next module, we start giving the agent whole new toolboxes from the outside world — connecting your first MCP server.

21
00:01:06,485 --> 00:01:07,000
See you there.

